From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: Offer only the AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80 SRTP profile

The device answers as the DTLS server and picks the first profile of its own list that the browser offers.
Upstream lists AEAD_AES_256_GCM and AEAD_AES_128_GCM first, so browsers got AES-256-GCM, and the frame
protector of patch 0001 (AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80 only) never ran: every RTP packet was protected by libsrtp
on its own and sent with its own system call, without the batch and UDP segmentation of patch 0003.
AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80 is the profile every WebRTC endpoint must support (RFC 8827, section 6.5).
---
--- a/src/impl/dtlstransport.cpp
+++ b/src/impl/dtlstransport.cpp
@@ -848,21 +848,13 @@
 		// RFC 8827: The DTLS-SRTP protection profile SRTP_AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80 MUST be supported
 		// See https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8827.html#section-6.5
 		// Warning: SSL_set_tlsext_use_srtp() returns 0 on success and 1 on error
-#if RTC_ENABLE_MEDIA
-		// Try to use GCM suite
-		if (!DtlsSrtpTransport::IsGcmSupported() ||
-		    SSL_set_tlsext_use_srtp(
-		        mSsl, "SRTP_AEAD_AES_256_GCM:SRTP_AEAD_AES_128_GCM:SRTP_AES128_CM_SHA1_80")) {
-			PLOG_WARNING << "AES-GCM for SRTP is not supported, falling back to default profile";
-			if (SSL_set_tlsext_use_srtp(mSsl, "SRTP_AES128_CM_SHA1_80"))
-				throw std::runtime_error("Failed to set SRTP profile: " +
-				                         openssl::error_string(ERR_get_error()));
-		}
-#else
+		// Only AES128_CM_HMAC_SHA1_80: NanoKVM is the DTLS server and takes the first profile of this list that the
+		// browser offers. With the AES-GCM profiles first every browser got AES-256-GCM, which the frame protector
+		// (one CryptoDMA request per frame, AES-CTR and SHA-1 chained per packet) does not take: each packet then
+		// went through libsrtp and was sent on its own, without the batch and UDP segmentation.
 		if (SSL_set_tlsext_use_srtp(mSsl, "SRTP_AES128_CM_SHA1_80"))
 			throw std::runtime_error("Failed to set SRTP profile: " +
 			                         openssl::error_string(ERR_get_error()));
-#endif
 	} catch (...) {
 		if (mSsl)
 			SSL_free(mSsl);
